<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.13.3: Finding Domain and Range Using a Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01096</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.13.3: Finding Domain and Range Using a Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>09375ce1-d937-49b5-9d4a-3161fddaefb4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>A tennis ball was dropped from a certain height. It bounced several times, rolled along for a short period, and then stopped. Function \(H\) gives its height over time.</para>
<para>Here is a partial graph of \(H\). Height is measured in feet. Time is measured in seconds.</para>
<para>Use the graph to help you answer the questions.</para>
<para>Be prepared to explain what each value or set of values means in this situation.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Nonlinear function. Time (seconds) and height (feet)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f263631beed5c32faad860eada6777c68e83f3b9"/></media></figure></para>


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Find \(H(0)\). What point is this on the graph? What does it represent?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(H(0)=6\). It tells us the ball was dropped from a height of 6 feet at \(t=0\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Solve \(H(x)=0\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(x=0.54\), \(x=1.48\), and \(x=2.25\) are three possible solutions, but there are others. Each one represents the time when the ball hit the ground.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Describe the domain of the function.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The domain includes all values between 0 and the time when the ball stopped moving, which, based on the graph, is about 3.8.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Describe the range of the function.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The range includes all values between 0 and 6 feet, the height from which the ball was dropped. </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<title>Video: Interpreting Function Notation</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more about function notation.</para>
